Example code for MTRX3760 Lab 2.

This folder holds two small, independent pieces you can build on, each with a
demonstration program, plus two example data files.

CRender.h and CRender.cpp are a supplied wrapper class around the raylib
graphics library. It hides raylib behind a small interface, so the rest of
your program never includes raylib.h and never sees a raylib type. The
instructions for installing raylib are at the top of CRender.h.

TestRender.cpp is a worked demonstration of using CRender: a screenful of
bouncing, tethered balls. It is an example to read, build and run, not a
starting template.

CLoopReader.h and CLoopReader.cpp are a supplied reader for loop files. A loop
file describes one closed loop as a name, a starting pose and a list of
vertices; CLoopReader reads such a file and stores what it finds. The file
format is described at the top of CLoopReader.h.

TestLoopReader.cpp is a worked demonstration of using CLoopReader together with
CRender: it loads a loop file and draws the loop on screen. Like TestRender, it
is an example, not a template.

SimpleWalls.map and SimpleLine.map are two example loop files. SimpleWalls.map
is the walls of a room; SimpleLine.map is a line on the floor, sitting inside
that room. TestLoopReader can load either one.

Getting going:

1. Install raylib. Instructions are inside CRender.h. There are two routes,
   one if you have sudo on your own machine and one for the lab machines,
   where you do not. Follow the one that applies to you.

2. Compile and run TestRender.cpp, then TestLoopReader.cpp. Build instructions
   are at the top of each file, and the exact build command depends on which
   install route you took.

Once that's working, you're all set to start designing and building the
program requested in the Lab 2 handout.
